course: dictionary
lesson: 1095
title: Reading with your fingertips: braille
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Braille is a system of raised dots that blind and partly sighted people read by touching them with their fingers. Each letter is a pattern of up to six dots, so a small set of dots can make every letter.
explain: Look at the context to understand it: «Fingertips are packed with touch sensors and can feel two points just 2 to 3 millimetres apart, while on the back two points a few centimetres apart can feel like one; that is why blind people read the tiny raised dots of Braille with their fingertips.» The sentence tells you that fingertips feel very small differences, so they are the best part of the body for reading dots.
explain: The word can be a noun, as in «read braille», or an adjective, as in «braille signs». Look at the words next to it to see which one it is. When you see a capital B, as in Louis Braille, it is a name.
fact: Braille is a system of raised dots that people read by touch.
fact: Blind and partly sighted people read braille with their fingertips.
fact: Each braille letter is a pattern of up to six dots.
fact: Louis Braille, a blind French boy, invented braille in the 1820s.
fact: You can find braille on lift buttons, medicine boxes and signs.
exercise: «Blind people read the tiny raised dots of Braille with their fingertips.» What do blind people read braille with? | *Their fingertips | Their ears | Their toes | Their eyes
exercise: Each braille letter is a pattern of up to six dots. How many dots can one braille letter have at most? | *Six | Ten | Three | Twelve
exercise: «Blind people read the tiny raised dots of Braille with their fingertips.» Find and copy one word that means lifted up above a surface. | tiny | dots | *raised | fingertips
test: What is braille? | A kind of music played on a drum | A sort of map showing roads and rivers | A type of lamp used in theatres | *A system of raised dots that blind and partly sighted people read by touch
test: Louis Braille, a blind French boy, invented braille in which decade? | The 1920s | The 1720s | *The 1820s | The 1620s
exam: Why do blind people read braille with their fingertips rather than the back of their hand? | The back of the hand is too cold to feel anything at all | *Fingertips can feel two points just 2 to 3 millimetres apart, while the back of the hand cannot tell them apart | Fingertips are bigger than the back of the hand | Braille dots are only found on fingers
